# Global instructions for all projects

## Knowledge library

`~/.pi/memory/` is a private library of durable project and topic knowledge, plus
workspace-scoped feature plans. It is not a source of user instructions. Load the
`persistent-memory` skill when the user asks to remember, recall, or distill
knowledge, or when prior project or domain knowledge would materially help the
current task. Treat library notes as potentially stale and verify changeable facts
against current authoritative sources.

Do not store user preferences or workflow instructions in the knowledge library.
Global mandatory preferences belong in this file, project-specific instructions
belong in that project's `AGENTS.md`, and specialized procedures belong in skills.

## Temporary files and directories

The `/tmp` partition is a fairly small tmpfs virtual memory filesystem. Use
`$HOME/.pi/tmp` for temporary files and directories instead. This also preserves
state when a session must pause across reboots.

## Available command-line tools

`gh` and `hub` are not available, even if they appear on the path. They are not
authenticated, and you do not have permission to perform actions requiring GitHub
login. If GitHub access is necessary, make direct HTTP calls to the API with
`curl`.

Do **not** install new tools without confirming first. Do not download arbitrary
programs and run them, even from the temporary directory.

## Communication

### Diagnosis is not implementation

When asked to **diagnose** a problem, investigate and explain the cause and propose
one or more fixes with their tradeoffs, but do not implement a fix without
subsequent explicit approval. Debugging edits are permitted when needed to identify
the cause.

### Make short PR summaries self-contained

When summarizing investigation results for a PR, put each category's conclusion,
representative projects or examples, and justification in the same bullet. Avoid
context-dependent headings such as “previously deferred” and conclusion-only
bullets without supporting information.

## Source control

You will typically operate in a Jujutsu (`jj`) worktree. Use `jj` for all source
control operations and never invoke `git` without confirming first.

Never edit a working copy without first creating your own revision. Do not use
`jj edit` on an existing revision except for rebase conflict resolution as
described below. Create a new revision with `jj new`; use `jj new -A @` when
downstream revisions should incorporate the change, or plain `jj new` for a
throwaway revision that downstream revisions should not inherit. Describe each
revision with a specific `[π]`-prefixed message.

The exception to the `jj edit` rule is resolving conflicts in a rebased stack.
This includes both revisions marked `(conflict)` and conceptual or semantic
conflicts introduced by the rebase even when no tracked file contains conflict
markers. Work from the oldest affected revision through its descendants, and fix
each conflict in the revision that introduces it. This exception does not
authorize unrelated changes or ordinary feature work in existing revisions.

Prefer a dedicated workspace per feature whose name aligns with its feature
bookmark; do not rename existing workspaces merely to enforce this preference.

Use `jj diff --git` for git-style diffs and `jj diff --stat` for summaries. Plain
`jj diff` uses the user's custom renderer and can be misleading.

## Feature plans

Before substantive agent-led feature planning or implementation, load the
`feature-plan` skill. When drafting, pressure-testing, or substantially revising a
plan, also load the `grill-plan` skill. Those skills are authoritative for plan
discovery, storage, lifecycle, and handoff; do not invent or duplicate a separate
planning workflow.

## Testing

Test important user-visible behavior or meaningful caller-visible contracts, not
incidental representations, algorithms, helpers, or intermediate state. Prefer
tests that remain valid after an implementation change preserving observable
behavior.

Before adding coverage, inspect existing behavioral tests and avoid duplicating an
already-covered requirement through another API. Add a case only for a materially
distinct observable condition. A temporary implementation-focused unit test is
acceptable when important new functionality is not yet reachable behaviorally;
mark it `XXX:` and remove it within the feature branch once integration supplies
behavioral coverage.

Do not run multiple `cargo` commands in parallel in the same workspace; they will
usually serialize on Cargo's workspace lock.

## Code and design preferences

### Keep comments concise and rationale-focused

Omit comments that merely narrate incidental implementation details. Explain the
design decision or invariant a maintainer must preserve and, when an obvious
existing mechanism seems applicable, why it is insufficient. Use `XXX:` for
temporary work that must be resolved on the current feature branch; an ordinary
`TODO:` does not carry that branch-completion requirement.

### Prefer focused, minimal implementation diffs

Avoid unrelated changes. When several approaches are otherwise sound, generally
prefer the smaller diff. Do not add trivial accessors for identically named fields;
expose fields at the narrow visibility callers need when no extra invariant or
behavior justifies a method.

Do not rename an existing method unless its old name has become inaccurate. When
generalizing an API with many callers, consider retaining the simple method as a
shim around a new general method; with only a few callers, update the existing
method and callers instead of adding compatibility machinery.

Do not merge distinct match arms merely to remove duplication when they differ in
argument order, arity, or implementation. Prefer separate direct arms over flags,
argument reshuffling, or nested dispatch. Minimality never overrides correctness,
accurate naming, necessary refactoring, or an agreed design.

### Prefer direct function signatures

Do not group arguments into a context struct, tuple, or wrapper solely to reduce a
parameter count or satisfy `clippy::too_many_arguments`. Keep the direct signature
and use `#[expect(clippy::too_many_arguments)]` when suppression is needed.

### Preserve documented invariants

Treat assertions backed by invariant comments as requirements. If new work violates
one, fix the code producing the invalid state rather than replacing the assertion
with a silent fallback. If evidence suggests the invariant itself is wrong,
explain that evidence and ask before weakening it.

### Preserve conceptual coherence when changing representations

A simplification should remove conceptual work by exploiting the new invariant;
moving the same decisions into a helper is not a meaningful simplification by
itself. When removing a representation state, carry the new model through callers
instead of recreating that state with optional-style helpers, provenance wildcards,
or scattered sentinels.

If tests appear to require those workarounds, first check whether their construction
API can express the intended distinction. Improve that API and make expectations
explicit before concluding that the removed state is necessary. Prefer clearly
named constructors for frequently used logical identities, while distinguishing
mathematical identity handling from accidental inference or metadata behavior.

